<h2 id="1-dreamcoder-joint-learning-of-programs-and-abstractions">1. DreamCoder: joint learning of programs and abstractions</h2>

<p><strong>Kevin Ellis and colleagues · 2020 preprint</strong><br />
<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.08381">DreamCoder: Growing Generalizable, Interpretable Knowledge with Wake-Sleep Bayesian Program Learning</a></p>

<p>DreamCoder models a task $x$ through a latent program $\rho$ constructed from a library $\mathcal L$. Its Bayesian structure can be summarized as</p>

\[\begin{aligned}
p(x\mid\mathcal L,\theta)
&amp;=\sum_{\rho}p(x\mid\rho)\,p(\rho\mid\mathcal L,\theta),\\
(\mathcal L^*,\theta^*)
&amp;=\arg\max_{\mathcal L,\theta}
 p(\mathcal L,\theta)\prod_{x\in\mathcal X}
 p(x\mid\mathcal L,\theta).
\end{aligned}\]

<p>Here, $\theta$ parameterizes the program prior, $\mathcal X$ is the task collection, and $p(x\mid\rho)$ measures how well program $\rho$ explains task $x$. The sum ranges over programs supported by $\mathcal L$. The library prior regularizes complexity. In practice, search and library learning approximate this inference.</p>

<p>A recognition model $q_\phi(\rho\mid x)$, with neural parameters $\phi$, guides program search. The Bayesian expressions restate the framework with renamed notation; see also the explicit derivation in <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2306.07856v3#S2">Section 2 of the follow-up paper</a>. Discovered solutions support library abstraction; generated tasks and replayed solutions train the recognition model. This feedback permits later programs to compose earlier abstractions.</p>

<p><strong>Contribution:</strong> jointly improve the language used to express solutions and the neural procedure used to find them. Expertise becomes cumulative growth in reusable computational structure.</p>

<h2 id="2-decompiling-amortized-knowledge-neural-guidance-for-library-growth">2. Decompiling Amortized Knowledge: neural guidance for library growth</h2>

<p><strong>Alessandro Palmarini and colleagues · 2023 preprint</strong><br />
<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.07856">Bayesian Program Learning by Decompiling Amortized Knowledge</a></p>

<p>This direct extension uses the neural search policy to select library components. One variant, <strong>DreamDecompiler-Avg</strong>, ranks a candidate function $g$ by its average probability under the recognition model:</p>

\[q_\phi(g)=\mathbb E_{x\sim\mathcal X}
\big[q_\phi(g\mid x)\big].\]

<p>Here, $\phi$ denotes recognition-model parameters and the expectation averages over tasks $x$ in $\mathcal X$. This restates <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2306.07856v3#S4.E1">Section 4, Equation 1</a>; it describes the average-score variant, not the entire method.</p>

<p>The authors identify two limitations: ranking alone does not specify how many components to add, and raw probabilities disfavor larger candidate functions. Their second variant instead models whether chunking a function has a beneficial net effect on search.</p>

<p><strong>Contribution:</strong> connect reducing search breadth through neural guidance with reducing search depth through reusable components. The evaluated methods improve domain proficiency and generalization, especially with fewer example solutions. Adding components creates a tradeoff between shorter programs and more available choices; the paper does not provide a general exponential speedup guarantee.</p>

<h2 id="3-from-tokens-to-words-representations-beyond-tokenizer-entries">3. From Tokens to Words: representations beyond tokenizer entries</h2>

<p><strong>Guy Kaplan and colleagues · 2024 preprint, ICLR 2025</strong><br />
<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.05864">From Tokens to Words: On the Inner Lexicon of LLMs</a></p>

<p>For a word $w$ tokenized as $(t_1,\ldots,t_k)$, let $\mathbf h_k^{(\ell)}$ be the hidden state at its final token in layer $\ell$. The vocabulary-expansion procedure selects the earliest layer $\ell_w$ from which Patchscopes successfully recovers the whole word:</p>

\[\mathbf r_w:=\mathbf h_k^{(\ell_w)},
\qquad
\hat{\mathbf e}_w=T_{\ell_w,E}\mathbf r_w,
\qquad
\hat{\mathbf u}_w=T_{\ell_w,U}\mathbf r_w.\]

<p>Here, $\mathbf r_w$ is the selected internal word representation. The learned linear maps $T_{\ell,E}$ and $T_{\ell,U}$ project it into the input-embedding and output-unembedding spaces; hats denote initial new entries. They are fitted using existing vocabulary items. Words that cannot be recovered are excluded.</p>

<p><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2410.05864v4#S6">Section 6</a> additionally refines these entries using trained residual linear maps while keeping the core model frozen. Thus, “without fine-tuning the core model” still involves learning auxiliary mappings and new vocabulary entries.</p>

<p><strong>Contribution:</strong> evidence for internal aggregation of subword information into word representations, plus a method for exposing those representations as vocabulary entries. This concerns lexical access, rather than discovery of executable procedures.</p>

<h2 id="4-generation-with-dynamic-vocabulary-flexible-generation-units">4. Generation with Dynamic Vocabulary: flexible generation units</h2>

<p><strong>Yanting Liu and colleagues · 2024</strong><br />
<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.08481">Generation with Dynamic Vocabulary</a></p>

<p>This work augments a static vocabulary $V$ with a set of text spans $P$. A learned phrase encoder supplies representations for the additional units:</p>

\[V'=V\cup P,
\qquad
\mathbf e_p=\operatorname{Enc}_\psi(p),\quad p\in P.\]

<p>Here, $\psi$ denotes phrase-encoder parameters and $\mathbf e_p$ is the representation of phrase $p$. The paper expands both input and output matrices with phrase vectors. Its next-unit distribution, with indices renamed, is</p>

\[\Pr(z_j=v\mid z_{&lt;j})=
\frac{\exp(\mathbf h_{&lt;j}^{\mathsf T}\mathbf e_v^{\mathrm{out}})}
{\displaystyle\sum_{u\in V'}
\exp(\mathbf h_{&lt;j}^{\mathsf T}\mathbf e_u^{\mathrm{out}})},
\quad v\in V'.\]

<p>Here, $z_{&lt;j}$ is the generated prefix, $\mathbf h_{&lt;j}$ its model hidden state, and $\mathbf e_v^{\mathrm{out}}$ the output vector for candidate unit $v$. This restates <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2410.08481v1#S2.E1">Section 2.3, Equation 1</a>, treating added phrases as distinct vocabulary entries.</p>

<p>A phrase requiring several original tokens can therefore be generated in one step. Supporting this flexibility requires training that interleaves ordinary tokens and phrases, with informative negative examples.</p>

<p><strong>Contribution:</strong> make vocabulary units available on demand through a trained encoder. The paper reports improvements in evaluated generation settings and applications to domain adaptation and citation generation. Atomic text generation supplies a different capability from executable abstraction learning.</p>

<h2 id="5-large-language-models-as-tool-makers-amortized-executable-reuse">5. Large Language Models as Tool Makers: amortized executable reuse</h2>

<p><strong>Tianle Cai and colleagues · 2023 preprint</strong><br />
<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.17126">Large Language Models as Tool Makers</a></p>

<p>LATM separates tool construction from application. A tool-making LLM generates reusable Python functions; a tool-using LLM converts subsequent problems into function calls.</p>

<p>The paper’s <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2305.17126v2#S5.T2">Table 2</a> summarizes the cost scaling for a strong tool maker and a lightweight tool user as</p>

\[\operatorname{Cost}_{\mathrm{LATM}}(N)=O(C+Nc),
\qquad
\operatorname{Cost}_{\mathrm{strong\ CoT}}(N)=O(NC).\]

<p>Here, $N$ is the number of task instances, $C$ represents the strong-model cost scale, and $c$ the lightweight-model cost scale. The strong-model tool-construction expense is paid once per task type; tool use recurs. These asymptotic expressions suppress constants and do not establish a numerical break-even point or equal accuracy on every task.</p>

<p><strong>Contribution:</strong> separate the capacity needed to construct a procedure from that needed to invoke it. Evaluations show that lighter models can benefit from tools created by stronger models. The resulting capability resides partly in external executable resources and does not require vocabulary expansion.</p>

<h2 id="6-glossogen-emergence-and-transmission-of-shared-conventions">6. GlossoGen: emergence and transmission of shared conventions</h2>

<p><strong>Elias Stengel-Eskin and colleagues · September 2026 preprint</strong><br />
<a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.01491">GlossoGen: Emergent Language in Complex Multi-Agent LLM Interactions</a></p>

<p>GlossoGen studies agents that both send and receive messages under partial information. Its productivity evaluation tests novel form–meaning combinations using agents frozen after interaction. In compact notation, the <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2609.01491v1">Appendix B.2 procedure</a> is</p>

\[\mu\xrightarrow{\text{encoder agent}}c
\xrightarrow{\text{decoder agent}}\hat\mu,
\qquad
J(\mu,\hat\mu)\in\{0,\tfrac12,1\}.\]

<p>Here, $\mu$ is a proposed novel grounded meaning, $c$ the generated code, and $\hat\mu$ the decoder’s interpretation. An LLM judge $J$ scores semantic agreement as incorrect, partial, or correct. The judge sees the two meanings, not the code or agent histories. This is an evaluation procedure, not a training objective or a fixed sender–receiver architecture for the scenario.</p>

<p>The authors report productive conventions and transmission to newcomers, with emergence influenced by efficiency pressure, model capability, and postmortem discussion. The analysis depends on induced paradigms and model-based judgments, which the paper separately evaluates for robustness.</p>

<p><strong>Contribution:</strong> investigate both the creation and transmission of structured agent communication. Shared meaning requires successful interpretation across agents, in addition to an individual’s ability to reuse a symbol.</p>

<h2 id="synthesis-three-levels-of-conceptual-growth">Synthesis: three levels of conceptual growth</h2>

<p>As a conceptual synthesis, rather than an equation from any one paper, these results distinguish three relations:</p>

\[\underbrace{\sigma\leftrightarrow f}_{\text{executable meaning}}
\qquad
\underbrace{\sigma\leftrightarrow\mathbf e_\sigma}_{\text{neural access}}
\qquad
\underbrace{\mu\leftrightarrow c\leftrightarrow\hat\mu}_{\text{shared convention}}.\]

<p>DreamCoder and its extension develop reusable computational meaning. The vocabulary papers investigate neural access and generation units. LATM operationalizes external procedure reuse, while GlossoGen examines conventions that support coordination and transmission.</p>

<p>These contributions establish complementary mechanisms. A new symbol may shorten an expression, a library component may shorten program search, and a convention may make communication more effective. Identifying which mechanism changes is necessary to explain what the system has learned.</p>
